1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,152
An airline just banned robots from buying your ticket.

2
00:00:03,202 --> 00:00:05,694
Even if you say yes to every step.

3
00:00:05,744 --> 00:00:09,462
On eleven November, United changed its contract of carriage.

4
00:00:09,512 --> 00:00:14,037
AI agents can no longer make, change or service a ticket for you.

5
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:18,534
Break the rule, and United says it can cancel your booking without telling you.

6
00:00:18,584 --> 00:00:20,528
Muja, are you worried?

7
00:00:20,578 --> 00:00:22,245
Yes, that part is real.

8
00:00:22,295 --> 00:00:25,661
Bookings made before eleven November will be honoured.

9
00:00:25,711 --> 00:00:28,341
A toy plane, Muja?

10
00:00:28,391 --> 00:00:34,836
Fun fact: the very first paid airline flight, in nineteen fourteen, had just one passenger.

11
00:00:34,886 --> 00:00:37,871
His seat sold at auction for four hundred dollars.

12
00:00:37,921 --> 00:00:38,847
Okay...

13
00:00:38,897 --> 00:00:40,315
back to our story.

14
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:41,112
Why?

15
00:00:41,162 --> 00:00:44,206
United says security and unintended purchases.

16
00:00:44,256 --> 00:00:50,749
Analysts say it is about control, and reports quote its CEO saying he does not want another middleman.

17
00:00:50,799 --> 00:00:52,414
So what should Priya do?

18
00:00:52,464 --> 00:00:54,937
Let AI research and compare.

19
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:57,729
Book on United's own site yourself.

20
00:00:57,779 --> 00:01:00,104
And never hand an agent your login.

21
00:01:00,154 --> 00:01:03,577
My take: part security, part who owns the customer.

22
00:01:03,627 --> 00:01:05,301
Keep the final click human.

23
00:01:05,351 --> 00:01:07,743
The full story is on my blog.
